# Off-machine pair: the USB disk's Backup folder to a NAS, once a day.
# Copy to ~/.config/system/mirror-sync/mirrors-nas.conf ($SYSTEM_TOOLS_CONFIG/mirror-sync) and set your own paths.
# /Volumes/Offsite is a NAS share mounted over SMB; it is the only
# destination here not on this Mac, so it is the copy that survives theft,
# fire or a dead Mac.
#
# Format matches mirrors.conf. Run this list ADDITIVE (MIRROR_SYNC_ADDITIVE=1):
# the NAS Backup folder may already hold history the USB disk does not have,
# such as older sd-db snapshots in sd-backups/. An exact mirror would delete
# that history on its first pass.
#
# /Volumes/local/Backup carries the nightly sd-db snapshot in sd-backups/,
# so this pair is also how that snapshot leaves the machine.
#
# A promoted satellite pairs its SD_DB_BACKUP_DESTINATION folder with the
# NAS's sd-backups folder, one folder to one folder, never a parent with the
# share: the share's other folders are not in the source (sd:4683).
#   /path/to/sd-backups|/Volumes/Offsite/Backup/sd-backups|runner-journal/*.lock
#
# The third field keeps the snapshot's runner journal locks off the NAS
# (sd:6048). They are empty files restore makes again; rsync's open of one
# failed the copy on 2026-10-24, and offsite-verify passes a copy without
# them. Leave mirrors-nas-hourly.conf without it: the NAS prune deletes an
# hourly copy only when its file names match its manifest.
/Volumes/local/Backup|/Volumes/Offsite/Backup|runner-journal/*.lock
